  "live_records": {
    "q-40943": {
      "dom_id": "step-q40943",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+2b+c?$",
      "worked_solution": "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 12 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 12 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 12 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}+{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}-4(\\frac{3}{2}+{x}_{3})+2{x}_{3}=-2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2+{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2+{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+2b+c=1+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+0=4.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
