    "q-40950": {
      "dom_id": "step-q40950",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,+\\,{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $2a-3b-6c?$",
      "worked_solution": "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(1/6){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=(1/2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable.  \n\nFrom the third row, we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{3}=1,\n$$\n\nand from the first row we obtain\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n2a-3b-6c=2(\\frac{1}{2})-3(1)-6(0)=-2.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
